{"text": "From: Victoria Hutter\n\nTo: Adam@openthebooks.com\nSubject: RE: your Forbes article\nDate: Tuesday, June 13, 2017 2:27:00 PM\nAttachments: image001.pna\n\nAdam: Thank you so much for your quick response and for making the changes. It is much\nappreciated.\n\n| would like to add that in 2015, 65 percent of NEA direct grants went to small and medium-sized\norganizations (with small organizations less than $350,000 in prior year expenditures and medium\norganizations with $350,000 to $1.75 million). The NEA funds non-profits for specific projects or\nproductions only. Proposals from applicants, regardless of organization size are reviewed on the\nbasis of artistic excellence and artistic merit by a panel of citizen experts convened by the NEA.\n\nPlease let me know if | can provide additional information.\nRegards,\n\nVictoria Hutter\nAssistant Director - Press | Public Affairs\nNational Endowment for the Arts\n400 7th Street SW | Washington DC 20506\nhutterv@arts.gov | 202-682-5692 (p) | 202-682-5611 (fax)\n\nLearn more about the arts in your community at arts.gov\n\nFrom: Adam Andrzejewski [mailto:Adam@openthebooks.com]\nSent: Tuesday, June 13, 2017 12:20 PM\nTo: Victoria Hutter <hutterv@arts.gov>\nCc: Adam Andrzejewski <Adam@openthebooks.com>\nSubject: Re: your Forbes article\n\nHi Victoria,\n\nThank you for reaching out. We took out both 'didn't' words and the paragraph now\naccurately reflects your email and statement:\n\nAfter Trump, Jr\u2019s tweet, the National Endowment of the Arts (NEA) was quick to\nclarify it had not funded this particular Shakespeare in the Park performance.\nHowever, the NEA disclosed its $630,000 in grants to NYSF since 2009. The NEA\nalso disclosed that it continued grantmaking to NYSF\u2019s other Public Theater project -\n\u201cNew York Voices\u201d at Joe\u2019s Pub \u2014 giving $25,000 in February.\n\nWe appreciate the transparency at NEA!",
